  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Crl.Rev.P./35/2016 on 12 March, 2021 Author: Rumi Kumari Phukan Bench: Rumi Kumari Phukan Gahc010106572016 The Gauhati High Court (High Court Of Assam, Nagaland, Mizoram And Arunachal Pradesh) Case No. : Criminal Revision Petition No.35/2016 1. M/S. Green India, Station Road (Near Railway Gate), Karimganj, Assar, Represented By Md. Shahabul Islam Choudhury @ Parul Choudhury, Son Of Abdul Fattah Choudhury, Permanent Resident Of Batarshi, P.S. & District Karimganj, Assam. ..... -Versus- 1. The State Of Assam. .... Advocate for the petitioner/accused No.2: Mr. K. N. Choudhury, Sr. Advocate, Mr. N. Anand, Mr. T. Deuri, Ms. L. Srivastav and Mr. H. L. Maurya. Advocate for the respondent: Ms. S. Jahan, Addl. P.P., Assam. Before Hon'Ble Mrs. Justice Rumi Kumari Phukan Date of hearing: 08.03.2021. Date of judgment: 12.03.2021. Crl.Rev.P./35/2016 on 12 March, 2021 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/46241990/ 1 P Judgement And Order (Oral)",
    "facts": "2. The present revision petition is preferred against the concurrent findings of the learned trial Court as well as the learned appellate Court, whereby the petitioner/accused No.2 was convicted under Section 16(1)(A-1) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 (hereinafter referred to as 'the Act') and sentenced to undergo simple imprisonment for six months and to pay a fine of Rs.5,000/- (rupees five thousands) only, in default simple imprisonment for another three months. 3. The prosecution case in brief is that on 17.06.2005, the Food Inspector of Kamrup visited the premises of M/s. Deepak Store at Barabazar (owner Deepak Dey), Pandu and suspecting to be adulterated, collected 9 bottles of food drink (orange drink of Shaad Brand), containing 180 ml. in each bottle from the total stock of 72 numbers of bottle, imported to India by M/s. Green India, Karimganj and seized the same in presence of the witnesses. 4. On 18.06.2005, the seized articles were sent to the Public Analyst, Assam for analysis, who in his report dated 28.07.2005 opined that the sample of the food drink (orange drink of Shaad Brand), is misbranded as per the provision of Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 and Rule 40 of the PFA Rules, 1955 (as amended) and after getting the necessary prosecution sanction from the Local Health Authority, the Food Inspector filed the offence report before the trial Court against two accused, namely, the vendor Deepak Dey, proprietor of M/s. Deepak Store, as the accused No.1 and M/s. Green India, Karimganj as the accused No.2 (importer), which was registered as CR No. 4302c/2005. Page No.# 3/10 5. On appearance of the accused persons before the learned trial Court, the copies of the offence report along with other documents were furnished to them and considering the materials available on record, charge under Section 16(1)(A-1) of the Act was framed and explained against both accused persons, to which they pleaded not guilty and claimed to be tried. 6. During the course of trial, the prosecution examined two witnesses and recorded the statement of the accused persons under Section 313 of the CrPC. The defence also examined two witnesses in their support. Being aggrieved by both the trial Court as well as the appellate Court, the present revision is preferred by the accused petitioner/accused No.2, on the grounds, inter alia, that the learned Courts below failed to appreciate the evidence on record and the points of law involved in this case, in true perspective and came to a erroneous finding. 12. Be it mentioned that the two accused persons faced the trial for the offence under Section 7 (16) of the PFA Act. The vendor Deepak Roy is the accused No. 1, from whose premises, namely, M/s Deepak Store, 9 bottles of food drink (orange drink-shaad brand) were seized, out of the total stock of 72 nos. of the bottles. As the bottles containing the name of the importer, namely, M/s Green India, Karimganj, so the offence report was submitted against both the accused persons and both of them stood the trial. The plea of the vendor was different than that of the importer and the learned trial Court as well as the appellate Court has dealt with the matter accordingly. So far as the plea of the vendor is concerned, he sought protection under Section 19 (2) of the PFA Act that as he has purchased the article from the distributor and sold it on the same condition, so he is well protected under the Act. The vendor also took another plea of violation of Section 10 (7) for not examining the independent witnesses and non-compliance of Section 13 (2), regarding service of notice about the report of Public Analyst.",
    "arguments": "Secondly it is contended that the learned trial Court had shifted the burden of proof on the distributor/wholesaler, which is bad in law and as such the impugned judgment is liable to be [LEAKAGE_MASK]. It is further contended that the concerned drinks were brought into India from Bangladesh after proper analysis by the Public Health Laboratory, which certified that the concerned product conforms the standards of food drink and prays for setting aside Page No.# 4/10 the impugned judgment of conviction and sentence and acquit accused petitioner from the said offence. 14. On the other hand, the learned appellate Court has given due consideration to the defence evidence produced by the accused petitioner No. 2 and also appreciated the evidence adduced by the prosecution as well as the defence side. According to Section 14 of the PFA Act, no manufacturer or distributor of, or dealer in, any article of food shall sell such articles to any vendor unless he also gives a warranty in writing in the prescribed form about the nature and quality of each article to the vendor.\" 16. The learned Senior counsel for the petitioner, Mr K N Choudhury, has vehemently attacked such finding of the appellate Court that despite the learned appellate Court gave a finding that the accused petitioner No. 2 has duly imported the sample as per the documentary evidence and he was permitted to sell the same by the custom also and there is a finding that the accused No. 1 did not purchase the article from the accused No. 2 and the accused petitioner cannot be convicted only because of the label attached to the sample bottle, sold by the vendor, bear the name of importer/petitioner No. 2. 17. Also heard the submissions of learned Additional Public Prosecutor, who has fairly submitted that one cannot be convicted merely on possibility that he may have misbranded the sample food- product. 19. Section 14 of the PFA Act reads as follows:- \"No (manufacturer or distributor or dealer) in any article of food shall sell such article to any such vendor unless he also gives a warranty in writing in the prescribed form about the nature and quality of such article to the vendor. [Provided that a bill, cash memorandum or invoice in respect of the sale of any article of food given by a manufacturer or distributor of, or dealer in, such article to the vendor thereof shall be deemed to be a warranty given by such manufacturer, distributor or dealer under this section.] Explanation- In this section, in sub-section (2) of section 19 and in section 20A, the expression \"distributor\" shall include a commission agent.]\" 21. Section 19 reads as follows:- \"19. Defences which may or may not be allowed in prosecutions under this Act.- (1) It shall be no defence in a prosecution for an offence pertaining to the sale of any adulterated or misbranded article of food to allege merely that the vendor was ignorant of the nature, substance or quality of the food sold by him or that the purchaser having purchased any Page No.# 9/10 article for analysis was not prejudiced by the sale. (2) A vendor shall not be deemed to have committed an offence pertaining to the sale of any adulterated or misbranded article of food if he proves- (a) that he purchased the article of food- (i) in a case where a licence is prescribed for the sale thereof, from a duly licensed manufacturer, distributor or dealer, (ii) in any other case, from any manufacturer, distributor or dealer, with a written warranty in the prescribed form; and Crl.Rev.P./35/2016 on 12 March, 2021 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/46241990/ 6 (b) that the article of food while in his possession was properly stored and that he sold it in the same state as he purchased it.] (3) Any person by whom a warranty as is referred to [in Section 14] is alleged to have been given shall be entitled to appear at the hearing and give evidence.\" Only the provision attributable to the importer is Section 5 of the PFA Act, which reads as follows:- \"Section-5- Prohibition of import of certain articles of food- No person shall import into India i) Any adulterated food, ii) Any misbranded food, iii) Any article of food for import of which a licence is prescribed, except in accordance of the conditions of licence, and Page No.# 10/10 iv) Any article of food in contravention of any other provision of this Act or any Rule made thereunder.\" 23.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "Secondly it is contended that the learned trial Court had shifted the burden of proof on the distributor/wholesaler, which is bad in law and as such the impugned judgment is liable to be [LEAKAGE_MASK]. It is further contended that the concerned drinks were brought into India from Bangladesh after proper analysis by the Public Health Laboratory, which certified that the concerned product conforms the standards of food drink and prays for setting aside Page No.# 4/10 the impugned judgment of conviction and sentence and acquit accused petitioner from the said offence.",
    "respondent_arguments": "14. On the other hand, the learned appellate Court has given due consideration to the defence evidence produced by the accused petitioner No. 2 and also appreciated the evidence adduced by the prosecution as well as the defence side. 16. The learned Senior counsel for the petitioner, Mr K N Choudhury, has vehemently attacked such finding of the appellate Court that despite the learned appellate Court gave a finding that the accused petitioner No. 2 has duly imported the sample as per the documentary evidence and he was permitted to sell the same by the custom also and there is a finding that the accused No. 1 did not purchase the article from the accused No. 2 and the accused petitioner cannot be convicted only because of the label attached to the sample bottle, sold by the vendor, bear the name of importer/petitioner No. 2. 17. Also heard the submissions of learned Additional Public Prosecutor, who has fairly submitted that one cannot be convicted merely on possibility that he may have misbranded the sample food- product.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "According to Section 14 of the PFA Act, no manufacturer or distributor of, or dealer in, any article of food shall sell such articles to any vendor unless he also gives a warranty in writing in the prescribed form about the nature and quality of each article to the vendor.\" 19. Section 14 of the PFA Act reads as follows:- \"No (manufacturer or distributor or dealer) in any article of food shall sell such article to any such vendor unless he also gives a warranty in writing in the prescribed form about the nature and quality of such article to the vendor. [Provided that a bill, cash memorandum or invoice in respect of the sale of any article of food given by a manufacturer or distributor of, or dealer in, such article to the vendor thereof shall be deemed to be a warranty given by such manufacturer, distributor or dealer under this section.] Explanation- In this section, in sub-section (2) of section 19 and in section 20A, the expression \"distributor\" shall include a commission agent.]\" 21. Section 19 reads as follows:- \"19. Defences which may or may not be allowed in prosecutions under this Act.- (1) It shall be no defence in a prosecution for an offence pertaining to the sale of any adulterated or misbranded article of food to allege merely that the vendor was ignorant of the nature, substance or quality of the food sold by him or that the purchaser having purchased any Page No.# 9/10 article for analysis was not prejudiced by the sale. (2) A vendor shall not be deemed to have committed an offence pertaining to the sale of any adulterated or misbranded article of food if he proves- (a) that he purchased the article of food- (i) in a case where a licence is prescribed for the sale thereof, from a duly licensed manufacturer, distributor or dealer, (ii) in any other case, from any manufacturer, distributor or dealer, with a written warranty in the prescribed form; and Crl.Rev.P./35/2016 on 12 March, 2021 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/46241990/ 6 (b) that the article of food while in his possession was properly stored and that he sold it in the same state as he purchased it.] (3) Any person by whom a warranty as is referred to [in Section 14] is alleged to have been given shall be entitled to appear at the hearing and give evidence.\" Only the provision attributable to the importer is Section 5 of the PFA Act, which reads as follows:- \"Section-5- Prohibition of import of certain articles of food- No person shall import into India i) Any adulterated food, ii) Any misbranded food, iii) Any article of food for import of which a licence is prescribed, except in accordance of the conditions of licence, and Page No.# 10/10 iv) Any article of food in contravention of any other provision of this Act or any Rule made thereunder.\" 23."
